---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<DocsLayout title="Typed GraphQL generation for Dart" description="Generate typed Dart models and operation documents from GraphQL schemas and operations.">
  <!--
    DIRECTION: Islands developer manual
    MODE: Read — generator concepts and code lead; the optional client stays secondary.
    WORLD: A durable IDE reference pane using the supplied Islands Dark scheme.
    SIGNATURE: Integration modes expose the actual generator boundary before installation.
    RESPONSIVE: Navigation becomes a compact section drawer while prose remains linear.
  -->
  <header class="home-intro">
    <p class="status">Prerelease · 0.1.0-alpha.7</p>
    <h1>GraphQL types for Dart.<br />Bring your own client.</h1>
    <p>Dartpollo Generator reads your schema and operations, then writes typed Dart models, variables, enums, fragments, and optional operation helpers. Use the generated output with any GraphQL transport—or add the Dartpollo client when its link and cache layer fit your app.</p>
    <div class="home-actions">
      <a class="primary-link" href={`${base}/getting-started/`}>Start with the generator</a>
      <a href={`${base}/integration-modes/`}>Choose an integration</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="mode-summary">
    <h2>Choose the boundary</h2>
    <div>
      <article>
        <h3>Models only</h3>
        <code>helpers: false · queries: false</code>
        <p>Generate JSON-serializable response and input types. Execute requests with your existing client and call the generated <code>fromJson</code>.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Models + documents</h3>
        <code>helpers: false · queries: true</code>
        <p>Also emit the operation <code>DocumentNode</code> and name so a compatible client can execute the exact generated operation.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Dartpollo helpers</h3>
        <code>helpers: true · queries: true</code>
        <p>Generate a <code>GraphQLQuery</code> subclass ready for the optional <code>DartpolloClient</code> and cached client.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>From operation to Dart</h2>
    <div class="file-pair">
      <div>
        <span>lib/graphql/viewer.graphql</span>
        <pre><code>query Viewer &#123;
  viewer &#123;
    login
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span>lib/__generated__/viewer.graphql.dart</span>
        <pre><code>final data = Viewer$Query.fromJson(json);

print(data.viewer.login);</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="path-list">
    <h2>Learn the system</h2>
    <a href={`${base}/getting-started/`}><strong>Getting started</strong><span>Install the generator, map a schema, and create the first output.</span></a>
    <a href={`${base}/configuration/`}><strong>Configuration reference</strong><span>Every top-level and per-schema option, default, and tradeoff.</span></a>
    <a href={`${base}/generated-output/`}><strong>Generated output</strong><span>Understand models, arguments, documents, helpers, and output paths.</span></a>
    <a href={`${base}/graphql-features/`}><strong>GraphQL features</strong><span>Fragments, custom scalars, enums, interfaces, mutations, and subscriptions.</span></a>
    <a href={`${base}/troubleshooting/`}><strong>Troubleshooting</strong><span>Fix missing files, scalars, fragments, roots, and duplicate class names.</span></a>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
